arXiv:2606.12876v1 [cs.LG] 11 Jun 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Multi-Bitwidth Quantization 
<br class="ltx_break">for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Liza Babaoglu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shuangyi Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ashish Khisti
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Toronto
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{liza.babaoglu, shuangyi.chen}@mail.utoronto.ca, akhisti@ece.utoronto.ca</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed across heterogeneous hardware with varying resource constraints, the ability to adaptively manage the trade-off between performance and efficiency without retraining is critical. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drop-by-Drop</span>, a novel <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-bitwidth</span> post-training quantization framework that enables inference-time precision control over LLM weights from a single trained model. Our method is theoretically grounded in <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">information theory</span> and <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">successive refinement</span>. We establish that LLM weights, which commonly follow a Gaussian distribution, can be optimally reconstructed with increasing fidelity as additional bits are incorporated, under a weighted mean squared error distortion motivated by LLM loss functions. To realize this in practice, Drop-by-Drop incorporates Matryoshka-style supervision into the loss function, exploiting the structure of additive codebooks. Drop-by-Drop produces a <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single</span> model where ordered subsets of codebooks yield accurate partial reconstructions at each precision level. This approach significantly reduces storage and memory overhead by allowing a single checkpoint to serve multiple bitwidths, while maintaining competitive perplexity and accuracy across major architectures, such as Qwen, LLaMA, Gemma, and Mistral.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid development of large language models (LLMs) has advanced natural language processing. Advancements in both algorithmic design and large-scale computational infrastructure have enabled open-access models such as LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Gemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Qwen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite> to achieve remarkable performance in language modeling, reasoning, summarization, information retrieval, and conversational interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naveed et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thompson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Despite their impressive capabilities, LLMs must ultimately be deployed for inference in real-world systems.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2606.12876v1/resourceaware.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:220/172;" width="220" height="172" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>LLM deployments</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Modern LLMs often contain tens or hundreds of billions of parameters, imposing substantial memory and computational demands. Deployment is particularly challenging across heterogeneous platforms that not only include large cloud servers but also personal devices and edge hardware with strict memory, latency, or power constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan &amp; Ding, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Quantization addresses part of this challenge by compressing model parameters into finite-precision representations, replacing high-precision floating point values. However, most quantization methods produce models at a fixed compression level, which is rarely optimal for all deployment settings. Since different platforms impose different resource constraints, maintaining multiple separately quantized model variants is often impractical due to storage and maintenance overhead. Motivated by this, we explore <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-bitwidth quantization</span> for LLM inference, where a single model can operate at multiple precision levels without retraining. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the multi-bitwidth model can activate all components for maximum accuracy or only a subset (e.g., <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) on resource constrained devices, enabling a smooth trade-off between accuracy and efficiency. Rather than committing to a fixed precision like the conventional method, the model can adjust its effective resource usage during inference to match the capabilities of the deployment device. Despite its practical significance, adaptive precision control during inference remains largely underexplored in LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From an information-theoretic perspective, multi-bitwidth quantization relates naturally to the concept of <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">successive source refinement</span>. In this setting, a source is encoded such that progressively adding more bits yield progressively more accurate reconstructions. Classical results show that certain sources are successively refinable under appropriate distortion measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Equitz &amp; Cover, 1991</a>)</cite>. However, to the best of our knowledge, the relationship between successive refinement theory and multi-bitwidth quantization methods has not been discussed in literature. In this work, we bridge this gap by connecting successive refinement theory with practical quantization techniques for LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To enable this precision control in LLMs, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drop-by-Drop</span>, a novel multi-bitwidth post-training quantization framework that allows inference-time control over the effective precision of model weights. Our method builds on AQLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egiazarian et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which represents weights as a sum of codewords drawn from multiple learned codebooks using additive quantization. While AQLM achieves strong accuracy at low bitwidths, it relies on a fixed number of active codebooks and therefore produces a static precision profile. Drop-by-Drop addresses this limitation by introducing Matryoshka-style supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kusupati et al., 2022</a>)</cite> during codebook training, explicitly encouraging intermediate subsets of codebooks to produce accurate <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partial</span> reconstructions. This design enables progressive compression by simply dropping codebooks during inference, allowing a single quantized model to operate across multiple resource budgets. Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Our Theoretical Contribution ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we establish a theorem proving that Gaussian sources are successively refinable under a weighted mean squared error distortion measure motivated by the loss functions used in LLMs. This extension of classical successive refinement theory motivates the design of our practical quantization framework introduced next.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Our Application: Drop-by-Drop (DbyD) ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drop-by-Drop</span>, a multi-bitwidth post-training quantization framework that enables inference-time precision control over LLM weights from a single trained model. By progressively dropping the additive codebooks, Drop-by-Drop adapts its precision, achieving a smooth tradeoff between model performance and bitwidth, as motivated by our information-theoretic foundations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Experiments ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we demonstrate that Drop-by-Drop maintains low perplexity and strong task accuracy across the full range of supported bitwidths, exhibiting graceful degradation in resource constrained settings without retraining or recalibration.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem Setting</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Information-Theoretic Preliminaries</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rate and Distortion.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> be a discrete random variable taking values in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability mass function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(w)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. Entropy of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the inherent uncertainty of the source and characterizes the minimum average number of bits required for lossless representation. It is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\mathsf{W})=-\sum_{w}p(w)\log p(w)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖶</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>w</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathsf{W})=-\sum_{w}p(w)\log p(w).</annotation></semantics></math> In the lossy setting, the source is encoded at rate <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> (in bits per source symbol) and reconstructed as <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The fidelity of this reconstruction is quantified by a distortion function
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:\mathcal{W}\times\widehat{\mathcal{W}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:\mathcal{W}\times\widehat{\mathcal{W}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and performance is measured by the expected distortion
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[d(\mathsf{W},\widehat{\mathsf{W}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[d(\mathsf{W},\widehat{\mathsf{W}})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> denote the maximum allowable expected distortion. For the distortion function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(w,\widehat{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(w,\widehat{w})</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}\times\widehat{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}\times\widehat{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the rate-distortion function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(D)</annotation></semantics></math> and its inverse <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(D)=\min_{p(\widehat{w}|w)}I(\mathsf{W};\widehat{\mathsf{W}})\quad\text{and}\quad D(R)=\min_{p(\widehat{w}|w):\,I(\mathsf{W};\widehat{\mathsf{W}})\leq R}\mathbb{E}\!\left[d(\mathsf{W},\widehat{\mathsf{W}})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(D)=\min_{p(\widehat{w}|w)}I(\mathsf{W};\widehat{\mathsf{W}})\quad\text{and}\quad D(R)=\min_{p(\widehat{w}|w):\,I(\mathsf{W};\widehat{\mathsf{W}})\leq R}\mathbb{E}\!\left[d(\mathsf{W},\widehat{\mathsf{W}})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The minimization in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is taken over all conditional probability distributions
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\widehat{w}\mid w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\widehat{w}\mid w)</annotation></semantics></math> that define a probabilistic mapping from the source symbol <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to its reconstruction <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to the distortion constraint
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w,\widehat{w}}p(w)\,p(\widehat{w}\mid w)\,d(w,\widehat{w})\leq D." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w,\widehat{w}}p(w)\,p(\widehat{w}\mid w)\,d(w,\widehat{w})\leq D.</annotation></semantics></math> The above expressions provide the single-letter characterization of the rate–distortion function. The associated operational definition, which considers block encoding and decoding of length-<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> source sequences and characterizes the achievable rate–distortion pairs in the limit as <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, can be found in standard information theory textbooks such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cover &amp; Thomas (2006, Ch. 10)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Successive Refinement.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A source <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{W},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{W},d)</annotation></semantics></math> is successively refinable if, for any finite distortion chain <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}&gt;D_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;D_{K}&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}&gt;D_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;D_{K}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists a single embedded code whose <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-prefix achieves the optimal rate–distortion pair <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R(D_{k}),D_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R(D_{k}),D_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math>, with no rate loss compared to encoding directly at <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, one code can operate optimally at multiple distortion levels. We define successive refinability for general sources in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Successive Refinability of General Sources). ‣ Successive Refinement. ‣ Appendix E Theoretical Foundations and Successive Refinement ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and for Gaussian sources under mean squared error (MSE) distortion in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Successive Refinability of Gaussian Sources). ‣ Successive Refinement. ‣ Appendix E Theoretical Foundations and Successive Refinement ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Loss Formulation for LLM Weight Quantization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are composed of stacked layers, each performing a linear transformation followed by a nonlinearity. In this work, we work on quantizing the LLM weights of a pre-trained model. For a given layer, let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathsf{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}\times d_{\mathrm{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathsf{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}\times d_{\mathrm{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote its weight matrix, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input and output dimensions of the layer, respectively. We have a fixed data matrix of
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}\times n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}\times n},</annotation></semantics></math>
collected from representative inputs. For the first layer, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to token embeddings of the dataset. Whereas, for subsequent layers, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of intermediate activations produced by preceding layers. The corresponding layer outputs are
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathsf{Y}}=\bm{\mathsf{W}}\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}\times n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathsf{Y}}=\bm{\mathsf{W}}\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}\times n}.</annotation></semantics></math> For convenience, the notation description is in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Notation Summary ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantized reconstruction</em> of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathsf{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathsf{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To evaluate the impact of quantization on the layer outputs, we define the <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weighted mean squared error (WMSE)</em>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{WMSE}}(\bm{\mathsf{W}},\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})=\frac{1}{n}\lVert(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})\bm{X}\rVert_{F}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>WMSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{WMSE}}(\bm{\mathsf{W}},\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})=\frac{1}{n}\lVert(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})\bm{X}\rVert_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}=\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}=\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity matrix, this reduces to the standard MSE between <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, normalizing by the output dimension yields the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{WMSE}}(\bm{\mathsf{W}},\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})=\frac{1}{d_{\mathrm{out}}n}\bigl\|(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})\bm{X}\bigr\|_{F}^{2}=\frac{1}{d_{\mathrm{out}}}\operatorname{tr}\Big[(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})\frac{1}{n}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})^{\top}\Big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>WMSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{WMSE}}(\bm{\mathsf{W}},\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})=\frac{1}{d_{\mathrm{out}}n}\bigl\|(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})\bm{X}\bigr\|_{F}^{2}=\frac{1}{d_{\mathrm{out}}}\operatorname{tr}\Big[(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})\frac{1}{n}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}(\bm{\mathsf{W}}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}})^{\top}\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background &amp; Related Work</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Quantization Techniques</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Post-training quantization (PTQ) is widely used for compressing LLMs, as it avoids the cost of retraining large networks. PTQ methods construct a low-precision model from a pre-trained network using a smaller calibration dataset. Although quantization aware training (QAT) often achieves higher accuracy, it is costly for LLMs which have billions of parameters. Established PTQ methods, such as LLM.int8() <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and AWQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, show that LLMs can achieve 4-bit or 8-bit weight precision with minimal accuracy loss. These techniques utilize per-channel scaling, second-order information, or activation-aware calibration to mitigate the impact of outliers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Residual quantization extends PTQ by introducing a secondary codebook that refines the output of a base quantization stage. Recent methods such as QuIP# <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tseng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and VPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> adopt this scheme for low-bit LLM compression: QuIP# employs lattice-structured codebooks with learned rotations and scaling, while VPTQ couples residual vector quantization with fine-tuning. However, despite their two-stage structure, these methods jointly optimize all components end-to-end and do not explicitly supervise the base stage in isolation. As a result, disabling the residual stage at inference time leads to substantial degradation, which we empirically observe for QuIP# in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B QuIP#’s Residual Quantization Codebook Dropping Results ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Adaptive methods such as early-exiting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, slimmable networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu &amp; Huang, 2019</a>)</cite>, and dynamic token pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rao et al., 2021</a>)</cite> adjust execution paths by selecting layers, channels, or tokens based on resource constraints, but do not adapt the numerical precision of model weights. Adaptive quantization methods aim to fill this gap: AdaBits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2020</a>)</cite> uses QAT to support multiple bitwidths via nested scalar quantization, while the PTQ methods ResQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saxena et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and LSAQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> perform mixed-precision and layer-aware quantization, respectively, distributing precision across layers rather than enabling selective codebook dropping. Other adaptive approaches target different applications entirely: implicit neural codebooks for similarity search such as Qinco/Qinco2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huijben et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vallaeys et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and adaptive-rate variational-autoencoders such as ARTOVeQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malka et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fishel et al., 2025</a>)</cite> require compute-intensive decoding that makes them impractical for real-time inference. CacheGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> addresses a different problem as well, focusing on streaming-aware compression of KV cache activations rather than weight quantization. Closest to our setting, recent multi-bitwidth methods obtain their precisions by upscaling the representation: Any-Precision LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite> uses a non-uniform scalar quantizer whose lower-bit models are nested prefixes of the higher-bit one, while AnyBCQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2025</a>)</cite> shares frozen binary bit-planes fit by a data-unaware loss, applying the data-aware objective only to scales in a later stage. Drop-by-Drop avoids both compromises: it builds on additive vector codebooks and keeps a single data-aware WMSE objective over all parameters jointly (discrete codes, codebooks, and scales), with droppability arising from Matryoshka-style supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Additive Quantization and AQLM</h3>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.12876v1/codebook.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:220/122;" width="220" height="122"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustration of additive codebook-based reconstruction</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Additive quantization (AQ) removes the strict ordering imposed by residuals by representing weights as <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sums</span> of multiple independently selected vectors drawn from learned codebooks.
These components are optimized jointly rather than sequentially, resulting in a more flexible representation with higher expressive capacity at extreme compression ratios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Originally developed for approximate nearest neighbor search, AQ improves approximation quality over standard product and scalar quantization by reconstructing each weight group as a sum of vectors from multiple codebooks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babenko &amp; Lempitsky, 2014</a>)</cite>. Building on this idea, AQLM applies multi-codebook additive PTQ to compress LLM weights, representing each weight tensor as a sum of quantized vectors selected from distinct codebooks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a linear layer with weight matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathsf{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathsf{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and data inputs <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, AQLM aims to find quantized weights <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimize the squared error between the original and quantized outputs:
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}}\;\left\|\bm{\mathsf{W}}\bm{X}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}\bm{X}\right\|_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0.280em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}}\;\left\|\bm{\mathsf{W}}\bm{X}-\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}\bm{X}\right\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math> In AQLM, the weight matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathsf{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathsf{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is split into groups of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive elements. Each group is approximated by the sum of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> vectors, each selected from a learned codebook <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2},\ldots,C_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2},\ldots,C_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each codebook contains <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> codewords, where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of bits used to index the codewords. The selection for each group is encoded by <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> one-hot vectors <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ijm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ijm}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ijm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ijm}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the codeword chosen from the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th codebook for the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th group in the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th output row. Formally, a group is reconstructed as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m=1}^{M}C_{m}b_{ijm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m=1}^{M}C_{m}b_{ijm}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the assignments <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> specify the combination of codewords whose sum reconstructs each weight group. Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Additive Quantization and AQLM ‣ 3 Background &amp; Related Work ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a high-level visual illustration of this process, showing how each weight group is reconstructed by summing selected codewords from multiple codebooks. To form the full quantized weight matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, AQLM concatenates the reconstructed groups along the input dimension, where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> denotes vector concatenation across the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{in}}/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{in}}/g</annotation></semantics></math> groups per output row:
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}_{i}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}C_{m}b_{i,1,m}\oplus\cdots\oplus\sum_{m=1}^{M}C_{m}b_{i,d_{\mathrm{in}}/g,m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bm{\mathsf{W}}}_{i}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}C_{m}b_{i,1,m}\oplus\cdots\oplus\sum_{m=1}^{M}C_{m}b_{i,d_{\mathrm{in}}/g,m}.</annotation></semantics></math> The optimization process of AQLM is described in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C AQLM’s Optimization Process ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. As a result, AQLM uses a fixed number of additive codebooks during inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Matryoshka Representation Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Matryoshka Representation Learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kusupati et al., 2022</a>)</cite> enables a single model to adaptively use representations at varying fidelity by encouraging intermediate prefixes to approximate the final output, supporting coarse-to-fine inference. Related work includes Matryoshka Query Transformer for Large Vision-Language Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which applies the same principle in multi-modal settings, and MatQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which extends Matryoshka supervision to quantized models via QAT across multiple bitwidths. We integrate Matryoshka supervision with AQLM’s additive codebooks via a loss function where partial sums of codebooks can yield meaningful approximations of the weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Our Theoretical Contribution</h2>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><img src="2606.12876v1/imgs/plots/March_1_FITTED_scaled_weight_hist_layer_2_Linear.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:247/138;" width="247" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Qwen-7B <span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">layer 0 self_attn.v_proj</span> weights fitted to a Gaussian distribution</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now characterize the <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">successive refinability of Gaussian sources under weighted MSE</em>, culminating in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Our Theoretical Contribution ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Empirically, layer weights are well-approximated by Gaussian distributions, as seen in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Our Theoretical Contribution ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Empirical Validation of the Gaussian Source Assumption ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, making the Gaussian source model analytically meaningful. At the same time, practical PTQ methods optimize data-aware weighted MSE objectives (WMSE), such as Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2 Loss Formulation for LLM Weight Quantization ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the weighting arises from the data matrix <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This provides the theoretical motivation for designing progressive and adaptive quantization schemes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
