<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>AQLM’s Optimization Process</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The quantization process in AQLM learns both the codebooks <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and the assignments <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> encodes which codewords from each codebook are selected to approximate groups of weights. Initialization begins with a residual K-means procedure. Firstly,
a standard K-means clustering is applied to the grouped weights to generate initial cluster centers and assignments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2010</a>)</cite>. Then, the quantization errors are computed as the differences between weights and their assigned centroids. Lastly, subsequent codebooks are iteratively initialized by clustering these <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">residual</span> errors, enabling each additional codebook to compensate for errors left by the previous ones. This hierarchical initialization helps achieve an effective residual-based representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Learning proceeds through iterative alternating optimization of the discrete codes and continuous codebooks, consisting of three main phases:</p>
<ol id="A3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code Optimization:</span> The discrete assignment vectors <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are refined using a beam search algorithm, which searches for combinations of codewords that minimize the quantization error between the original and reconstructed weights. This step focuses on minimizing the error in model outputs induced by quantization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codebook Update:</span> Given fixed codes, the codebooks <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are updated by solving a least squares problem that minimizes the difference between the quantized weights and the original full-precision weights. This gradient-based step optimizes the continuous parameters of the codebooks to better fit the data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intra-Layer Fine-Tuning:</span> Finally, to further reduce residual quantization errors, AQLM performs fine-tuning at the transformer block level. This phase <span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">jointly</span> adjusts the non-quantized model parameters and the codebooks, ensuring that the outputs of the quantized model closely match those of the original <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen &amp; Khisti, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimating Model Size.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the practical memory footprint of an AQLM-quantized model, we estimate the total storage cost for a given codebook configuration. The memory required for a quantized weight matrix comprises three primary components: the codebooks, the discrete codes, and the per-unit scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for a weight matrix with input dimension <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{in}</annotation></semantics></math>, output dimension <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{out}</annotation></semantics></math>, and group size <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, using <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> codebooks corresponding to <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-bit codes, the total amount of memory (in bits) is calculated as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codebooks:</span> <math id="A3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\cdot M\cdot 2^{B}\cdot 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\cdot M\cdot 2^{B}\cdot 16</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codes:</span> <math id="A3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{out}\cdot(d_{in}/g)\cdot B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{out}\cdot(d_{in}/g)\cdot B</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scales:</span> <math id="A3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{out}\cdot 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{out}\cdot 16</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">average bits per parameter</span> (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{b}</annotation></semantics></math>) is then computed by dividing the total size in bits by the total number of parameters:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{b}=\frac{\text{size in bits}}{\text{number of parameters}}=\frac{16*g*M*2^{B}+d_{out}*(d_{in}/g)*B+16*d_{out}}{d_{out}*d_{in}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>size in bits</mtext><mtext>number of parameters</mtext></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{b}=\frac{\text{size in bits}}{\text{number of parameters}}=\frac{16*g*M*2^{B}+d_{out}*(d_{in}/g)*B+16*d_{out}}{d_{out}*d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As a concrete example, consider the <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mlp.gate_proj</span> layer of a Llama 2 70B model (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{in}=8192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{in}=8192</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{out}=28672" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>28672</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{out}=28672</annotation></semantics></math>). With a group size <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=8</annotation></semantics></math> and two 8-bit codebooks (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2,B=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2,B=8</annotation></semantics></math>), Equation <a href="#A3.E2" title="In Estimating Model Size. ‣ Appendix C AQLM’s Optimization Process ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> yields approximately <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.002</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.002</annotation></semantics></math> bits per parameter. Typically, the storage cost is dominated by the codes, while the codebooks and scales induce a relatively small memory overhead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egiazarian et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Empirical Validation of the Gaussian Source Assumption</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix D Empirical Validation of the Gaussian Source Assumption ‣ Multi-Bitwidth Quantization for LLMs Using Additive Codebooks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> provides empirical evidence supporting the Gaussian modeling assumption used in our theoretical analysis. We show the distributions of LLM weight parameters from the first three transformer layers of <span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span>. Each layer contains seven weight matrices (four attention and three MLP projections). Across all modules, the distributions closely follow Gaussian profiles, supporting the use of Gaussian source models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="A4.F9.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:127.2pt;"><img src="2606.12876v1/imgs/plots/March_1_FITTED_scaled_weight_hist_layer_0_Linear.png" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:141/79;" width="141" height="79" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="A4.F9.2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Layer 0: <span id="A4.F9.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.0</span></p><img src="2606.12876v1/imgs/plots/March_1_FITTED_scaled_weight_hist_layer_1_Linear.png" id="A4.F9.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:141/77;" width="141" height="77" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="A4.F9.2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Layer 0: <span id="A4.F9.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.0</span></p><img src="2606.12876v1/imgs/plots/March_1_FITTED_scaled_weight_hist_layer_2_Linear.png" id="A4.F9.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:141/79;" width="141" height="79" alt="Refer to caption">
